  "worked_solution": "Let $A$ be the event that the chosen student plays basketball, and let $B$ be the event that the student is a boy. Then ${B}^{\u2032}$ is the event that the student is a girl.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected student plays basketball, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(A)=P(A|B)P(B)+P(A|{B}^{\u2032})P({B}^{\u2032})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $\\frac{3}{5}$ of the students are boys and $\\frac{2}{5}$ are girls, we have\n\n$$\nP(B)=\\frac{3}{5},\\,P({B}^{\u2032})=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\frac{1}{12}$ of the boys play basketball, we have\n\n$$\nP(A|B)=\\frac{1}{12}.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected student plays basketball is $\\frac{1}{15},$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=\\frac{1}{15}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(A|{B}^{\u2032}),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A) & =P(A|B)P(B)+P(A|{B}^{\u2032})P({B}^{\u2032}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{15} & =(\\frac{1}{12})(\\frac{3}{5})+P(A|{B}^{\u2032})(\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\ \\frac{1}{15} & =\\frac{1}{20}+\\frac{2}{5}P(A|{B}^{\u2032}) \\\\ 60\\cdot (\\frac{1}{15}) & =60\\cdot (\\frac{1}{20}+\\frac{2}{5}P(A|{B}^{\u2032}))\u200b \\\\ 4 & =3+24P(A|{B}^{\u2032}) \\\\ 1 & =24P(A|{B}^{\u2032}) \\\\ P(A|{B}^{\u2032}) & =\\frac{1}{24}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected girl plays basketball is $\\frac{1}{24}.$",
